# LMDS Source-Available Licence

**Version 1.0** · มีผลตั้งแต่ 20 กันยายน 2026 / in force from 20 September 2026

Software: **LMDS · Local Model Deploy Studio** (repository `AutoDeployDGXProject`)
Licensor: **neronain** — https://www.facebook.com/neronain.minidev
Copyright (c) 2026 neronain. All rights reserved.

---

## 0. The short version

**One machine is free. Any purpose, including business use. No licence file, no
registration, no activation, no payment details, nothing to ask us for.**

**Two or more machines managed together needs a commercial licence** — and that
is also where the software asks for one. The legal line and the technical line
are the same line on purpose: if LMDS runs for you, you are licensed for what
it is doing.

**Want to try a fleet first?** A free 30-day trial licence unlocking unlimited
machines is **issued automatically on request** — no human in the loop, no
payment details (§4). You will need one to evaluate stacked multi-machine
deployment, because that needs two machines by definition.

| What you are doing | What you need |
|---|---|
| Anything at all, on one serving machine — hobby, study, or running your company on it | Nothing. It is free. |
| Evaluating a fleet, or trying stacked deployment | A free 30-day trial — self-service |
| Running two or more serving machines managed together | A commercial licence |
| Delivering services to third parties on LMDS, at any scale | A commercial licence |
| Redistributing, reselling, rebranding or sub-licensing LMDS itself | A commercial licence — and see §8 |
| Wanting the recipe catalog and a support channel while staying on one machine | **Just a subscription. No machine-count licence needed** (§6) |

This table is a summary. Sections 1–16 are the agreement.

---

## 0.1 Why the source is public but this is not open source

The repository is public because we want you to read it, audit it, and try it
without asking anyone's permission. Security teams should be able to see
exactly what a tool does before it touches their GPUs, and the licence
enforcement in LMDS is deliberately readable for that reason.

Public does not mean unowned. You may **use** LMDS freely within the limits
below, and you may **modify your own copy**. You may not **fork it,
redistribute it, rebrand it, or resell it**. Those two things are not in
tension and nothing here is a trap: use is generous and explicitly free;
taking the generator itself and shipping it as yours is not permitted at any
tier.

Bundles LMDS generates for you are a separate matter and they are entirely
yours — see §9.

---

## 1. Definitions

### 1.1 "serving machine" — the term the whole licence turns on

A **serving machine** is a distinct computer — physical or virtual — on which
LMDS is installed and **is able to run a model**.

LMDS determines this itself, from what is actually present on the machine
rather than from its name or configuration. A machine is a serving machine if
it has either:

- a working `llama-server` executable, **or**
- Docker together with at least one NVIDIA GPU.

A machine with neither **cannot run a model and does not count**. LMDS calls
such a machine a **control plane**, and refuses model-serving operations on it
for operational reasons that have nothing to do with licensing.

**Counting rules.**

| Situation | Counts as |
|---|---|
| A physical computer that can serve | **1** |
| A control-plane machine with no usable engine — where you plan, generate bundles, push from, and open the web console | **0 — it is free and does not count** |
| A computer with 8 GPUs in it | **1.** GPU count is irrelevant; machines are counted, not accelerators |
| Several containers serving models on one machine | **1.** Containers are not machines — LMDS's own deployments are containers |
| A virtual machine or cloud instance that can serve | **1**, counted separately from its host and from every other VM on that host |
| Eight serving VMs on one physical box | **8.** Virtualising does not reduce the count |
| An ephemeral or autoscaled instance | **1 while it exists.** See 1.2 |
| Replacing a machine rather than adding one | **1.** See 1.4 |

**So the common free setup is: a GPU-less hub plus one serving node. That is
one machine, and it is free.**

### 1.2 How the count is measured

Your machine count is the **greatest number of serving machines able to serve
at the same time**. It is a concurrent measure, not a cumulative one.

Starting one cloud instance, finishing with it, destroying it, and later
starting another is **one** machine. Running five at once is **five**.

### 1.3 The limit is on machines managed together

The limit in §2 counts serving machines that are **managed together** — that
is, machines administered as one fleet: added to a common inventory, deployed
to, pushed to, cloned between, monitored, or otherwise operated from one place
or as one system.

**Two independent installations, each managing only its own single machine,
are each within the free tier.** Two people each running LMDS on their own box
owe nothing, and neither does one person running two boxes that never talk to
each other and are administered entirely separately.

This is a real and deliberate limit on the rule, not an oversight. Anyone who
actually wants a cluster — or simply wants to manage more than one machine
from one place, which is most of what LMDS is for — needs the machines managed
together, and that is where the licence applies.

### 1.4 Replacement, not addition

If you decommission a serving machine and stand up another in its place, that
is a replacement and your count does not increase, provided the machine being
replaced stops serving within **30 days**. Hardware fails and gets replaced;
that is not an upgrade event and we will not treat it as one.

### 1.5 Honesty about the role check

LMDS provides `LMDS_ROLE=hub` so you can correct the detection when it guesses
wrong — for example on a machine where you are about to build an engine but
have not yet. That override exists to make the software behave correctly.

**Using it to make a machine that actually serves models not count is a breach
of this licence, even though it works.** The measure in 1.1 is what the
machine actually does, not what it reports. LMDS records in its own inventory
whether a role was forced, so this is visible rather than secret.

### 1.6 Noticing other LMDS installations on your network

LMDS may notice that another LMDS installation is present on the same local
network and tell you so — for example, *"found another LMDS at 10.0.0.5"*.

Three things about this are binding terms, not product behaviour that could
change:

1. **It is local detection only. Nothing is reported to the licensor.** No
   information about your machines, your network, your models or your usage is
   sent anywhere. LMDS has no telemetry, this licence introduces none, and
   nothing in this section is a channel for any.
2. **It is information, not enforcement.** It does not block anything, does not
   change your machine count, and does not alter what you are permitted to do.
3. **Finding another installation does not mean you are over the limit.**
   Separate installations that are not managed together are compliant under
   §1.3 — two independent installs on one network are each free. The notice
   means only that another LMDS is present. It is a prompt to check whether
   you would like them to work as one fleet, which is a licensed feature and a
   reason to talk to us, and it is never a statement that you are in breach.

---

### 1.7 Other terms

**"the Software"** means LMDS · Local Model Deploy Studio, its source code,
templates, recipe catalog and documentation, and any part of them.

**"you"** means the person or organisation exercising rights under this
licence. If you act for an organisation, "you" includes that organisation, and
machines managed together are counted across the whole organisation.

**"bundle"** means the deployment artefacts the Software generates —
controller scripts, `README.md`, `MODEL_PROFILE.yaml`, `SPECIAL_FILES.md`,
checksum manifests and the other files written into a generated deployment
directory.

**"commercial licence"** means a separate written agreement signed with the
licensor. See `COMMERCIAL.md`.

**"trial licence"** means a time-limited licence file issued under §4.

**"subscription"** means the recipe catalog and support subscription described
in §6. A subscription is not a machine-count licence and does not change your
machine limit.

